  "body": "﻿## What happens\n\nIn the simulation playback of a rotary program (a rotary that turns the work about a horizontal line, e.g. a Rotary Flow on a mill-turn with an A rotary), an indexed/posed station is drawn with the whole tool assembly - shank, holder, spindle housing - rotating about the TOOL TIP, while the work stays at its document pose. The tool visibly swings from radial to pointing down, driving the 250 mm housing far below the table.\n\nThe playback timeline carries only a tool tip and a tool-axis vector (`crates/sim/src/sim.rs:188-218`, `:602-632` - a posed move is stored as an A/B/C letter, never a work transform), and the renderer poses the tool about the tip with a shortest-rotation map (`crates/app/src/tool_body_view.rs:151-177`), reading no rotary axis line (`crates/ui/src/egui_app/world.rs:313-348`).\n\n## Why it matters\n\nThe drawn motion is kinematically wrong for a work-turning rotary: the machine turns the WORK about the rotary line while the tool stays upright. The playback shows the opposite, which reads as a collision that does not exist and hides the one that could. Display-only today (the post gate runs its own math), but it trains the user's eye on the wrong kinematics.\n\n## Suggested behaviour\n\nAt an indexed pose, draw the work rotated about the stated rotary axis line and the tool upright (or at minimum, draw the tool upright at the tip station and note the simplification), matching the machine-frame kinematics the gate judges with.\n\nSuggested labels: P7, area:ui, effort:medium, source:agent.\r\n",
